        "thread_id": "TH1",
        "thread_goal": "Align on current sorting workflow, main bottlenecks and safety pain points.",
        "messages": [
          {
            "msg_id": "M1",
            "speaker_id": 204917,
            "reply_to": null,
            "text": "Team, let's kick off the sorting line upgrade project. To start, can everyone share their view on the current workflow? What are the biggest daily frustrations and bottlenecks you see? I'm starting the map for T1.",
            "mentions": [
              31940,
              41356,
              307206,
              468833,
              489662
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "T1"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M2",
            "speaker_id": 307206,
            "reply_to": "M1",
            "text": "The biggest issue for me is the pile-up of unsorted materials right at the start of the line, especially on morning shift. It blocks the walkway.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "arrow_up",
                "count": 2
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M3",
            "speaker_id": 489662,
            "reply_to": "M2",
            "text": "Agree with that. And when the forklift comes through, it's a tight squeeze. We have to stop sorting to let it pass safely.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M4",
            "speaker_id": 31940,
            "reply_to": "M3",
            "text": "That forklift path is a major hazard. It crosses directly where people are walking with bins. A simple painted line isn't enough. Another key bottleneck is how we handle unexpected hazardous items – everything stops while we figure out what to do with a leaking container or old battery.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "warning",
                "count": 1
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M5",
            "speaker_id": 468833,
            "reply_to": "M4",
            "text": "Exactly. The lack of a clear, immediate spot for hazardous waste is a big one. It also increases contamination risk if someone just throws it in the wrong bin to keep the line moving. That's a compliance issue waiting to happen.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M6",
            "speaker_id": 204917,
            "reply_to": "M5",
            "text": "Good points. I've added these to the T1 notes: 1. Pile-ups at intake. 2. Forklift/pedestrian conflict. 3. No process for unexpected hazmat. Anything else?",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "T1"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "+1",
                "count": 3
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M7",
            "speaker_id": 41356,
            "reply_to": "M6",
            "text": "the bins get full too fast sometimes",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M8",
            "speaker_id": 307206,
            "reply_to": "M7",
            "text": "Yeah, especially the plastics bin. It's bulky but light, so it fills up before the shift change.",
            "mentions": [],
